# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Bandura
M.append(lesson_entry(
 key="personal-training-cap", name="Albert Bandura", min=28,
 outcome="Map each week of an 8-week plan to a source of self-efficacy, with the strongest source first, and use it to defend your coaching file.",
 intro="Albert Bandura's 1977 paper names four sources of a person's belief that they can act, and ranks them. Performance accomplishments come first and verbal encouragement alone is weakest. That ranking decides how an 8-week plan should be built.",
 record=["We read Bandura's 1977 paper in full text, in the sections on efficacy expectations and outcome expectations, the four sources, and the evidence on modelling, persuasion and arousal. It is a theory paper about treating fears and avoidance, and its experiments are mostly on phobias. {{bandura77}}"],
 steps=["Separate two expectations. An efficacy expectation is the conviction that you can do what it takes; an outcome expectancy is the belief that a given behaviour leads to a result. The paper argues the first is what drives action. {{bandura77}}",
        "Start with performance accomplishments. He calls this source especially influential because it is based on personal mastery experiences. Successes raise mastery expectations and repeated failures lower them, particularly early on. {{bandura77}}",
        "Expect some failure. After strong expectations are built by repeated success, occasional failures matter less, and failures later overcome by determined effort can strengthen persistence. {{bandura77}}",
        "Add models, but know they are weaker. Vicarious experience is a less dependable source than one's own accomplishments, so expectations from modelling alone are likely to be weaker. Models who struggle and overcome help phobics more than those who perform easily, and similarity to the model helps. {{bandura77}}",
        "Use verbal persuasion with care. He says it is widely used because it is easy, but expectations induced this way are likely to be weaker than those from one's own accomplishments, because they lack an authentic experiential base and can be extinguished by a disconfirming experience. {{bandura77}}",
        "Read the body. Emotional arousal is informative: high arousal usually debilitates performance, so people expect success when not beset by aversive arousal. Persistence in activities that feel threatening but are in fact relatively safe produces mastery experiences that raise self-efficacy. {{bandura77}}"],
 notes=["Bandura's 1977 paper is about behavioural change and therapy for fears. Using the four sources to structure a coaching plan is our adaptation. Check any claim about a client's progress against the evidence in their file. {{bandura77}}"],
 table=("The four sources, Bandura's strength ordering, and an 8-week plan (the last column is our adaptation)", ["Source", "Bandura on its strength", "Where it goes in 8 weeks"],
        [["Performance accomplishments {{bandura77}}", "Especially influential; based on mastery", "Weeks 1 to 2 and again weeks 5 to 8: planned wins, then graded challenge"], ["Vicarious experience {{bandura77}}", "Weaker alone; better with similar models who struggle and overcome", "Week 3: a similar client's story or video, with permission"], ["Verbal persuasion {{bandura77}}", "Weaker than accomplishments; needs an experiential base", "Week 4: specific feedback tied to something the client has done"], ["Physiological states {{bandura77}}", "High arousal debilitates; reading it calmly helps", "Weeks 5 to 6: teach effort and breathlessness as normal"]],
        "The first two columns follow the paper. The last column is our adaptation."),
 worked=[("Worked example: mapping weeks to sources (illustrative)", "Eight weeks, strongest source first",
          "Weeks 1 and 2: easy wins, so the client banks early accomplishments. Plan each session so the client can complete what was set. Early failure counts for more. {{bandura77}}",
          "Week 3: introduce a model, such as a short demonstration or a story of a similar client who struggled and persisted. {{bandura77}}",
          "Week 4: give specific feedback and name progress, tied to what the client did. {{bandura77}}",
          "Weeks 5 and 6: increase challenge in steps, so mastery continues, and coach the reading of effort and breathlessness. {{bandura77}}",
          "Weeks 7 and 8: review the log with the client and plan beyond week 8."),
         ("Worked example: tracking planned wins (invented numbers)", "A success log",
          "Count sessions where the client completed all of what was planned. Weeks 1 to 4: 3 of 3, 3 of 3, 2 of 3, 3 of 3.",
          "That is 11 of 12 sessions, or 92%. A target such as 80% or more in the first two weeks is our choice, based on the point that early failure weighs more. {{bandura77}}",
          "If week 2 had been 1 of 3, cut the next week's load so the client can finish what is planned, rather than praise effort alone, which is a weaker source. {{bandura77}}",
          "Record each week's figure in the file as evidence for your choices.")],
 apply=("Apply it: defend the plan", ["List the eight weeks and the source each leans on.", "Make sure weeks 1 and 2 are set up for completed sessions.", "Write one sentence per week saying why you chose it.", "Add the success log and the target.", "Review the file at week 4 and change anything the log contradicts."]),
 act="Map your 8-week plan to the four sources, strongest first, and write one sentence per week defending the choice.",
 checks=[("Which is a source of self-efficacy in Bandura's model?", ["Performance accomplishments", "Equipment cost", "Weather", "Session length"], 0, "He names performance accomplishments, vicarious experience, verbal persuasion and physiological states. {{bandura77}}"),
         ("According to Bandura, why is verbal persuasion weaker than performance accomplishments?", ["It is expensive", "It lacks an authentic experiential base and can be extinguished by a disconfirming experience", "It is illegal", "It is slow"], 1, "He says persuasion-induced expectations are weaker because they lack an experiential base. {{bandura77}}"),
         ("In the success log, 11 of 12 sessions completed is about what percentage?", ["75%", "85%", "92%", "100%"], 2, "11 ÷ 12 = 0.917.")],
 take=["Build confidence from mastery first. {{bandura77}}", "Models and encouragement help, but are weaker alone. {{bandura77}}", "Log completed sessions as evidence for your plan."],
 quiz=("Which is a source of self-efficacy in Bandura's model?", ["Performance accomplishments", "Equipment cost", "Weather", "Session length"], 0, "Performance accomplishments, vicarious experience, verbal persuasion and physiological states. {{bandura77}}")))

